1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,288
The 1915 Singapore Mutiny:

2
00:00:02,368 --> 00:00:06,375
The Week a Regiment
Rose on Chinese New Year

3
00:00:06,375 --> 00:00:09,692
On the afternoon of the
15th of February 1915,

4
00:00:09,772 --> 00:00:14,860
which was Chinese New Year, about
800 Indian soldiers of the 5th Light

5
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:19,217
Infantry were stationed at
Alexandra Barracks, some of them

6
00:00:19,297 --> 00:00:23,875
guarding German prisoners of war
in the middle of a world war.

7
00:00:23,875 --> 00:00:26,729
By evening, some of them had turned their

8
00:00:26,809 --> 00:00:30,100
rifles on their own
officers and on civilians.

9
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:32,415
It took the better part of a week,

10
00:00:32,495 --> 00:00:36,650
and warships from Japan, France
and Russia, to put it down.

11
00:00:36,650 --> 00:00:41,533
The 5th Light Infantry was a
Muslim regiment of the Indian Army,

12
00:00:41,613 --> 00:00:45,025
made up largely of
Rajput and Pathan sepoys.

13
00:00:45,025 --> 00:00:49,052
It reached Singapore in
October 1914 and settled into

14
00:00:49,132 --> 00:00:53,468
Alexandra Barracks under
Lieutenant-Colonel E. V. Martin,

15
00:00:53,548 --> 00:00:57,500
whom several accounts
describe as a weak commander.

16
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:00,377
In January 1915 the men were told

17
00:01:00,457 --> 00:01:03,325
they would be sent to Hong Kong.

18
00:01:03,325 --> 00:01:07,150
Rumours spread that this was a
cover story and that they were

19
00:01:07,230 --> 00:01:10,606
really bound for the front
against the Ottoman Empire,

20
00:01:10,686 --> 00:01:14,975
whose sultan had called on Muslims
to fight Britain and its allies.

21
00:01:14,975 --> 00:01:19,407
A court of inquiry later blamed a
mix of causes: the Ottoman call,

22
00:01:19,487 --> 00:01:23,167
seditious propaganda circulated
by the Ghadar movement,

23
00:01:23,247 --> 00:01:27,063
discord within the regiment
itself, and the fact that the

24
00:01:27,143 --> 00:01:31,450
sepoys had been guarding a camp
of German prisoners at Tanglin.

25
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:35,468
Historians still argue about
how much weight each deserves,

26
00:01:35,548 --> 00:01:38,524
and about whether it
was a religious revolt,

27
00:01:38,604 --> 00:01:43,675
a nationalist one or an argument among
soldiers over their own treatment.

28
00:01:43,675 --> 00:01:48,725
At about 3.30 pm, four
companies of the regiment rose.

29
00:01:48,725 --> 00:01:52,799
Two British officers, Captain
Boyce and Lieutenant Elliott,

30
00:01:52,879 --> 00:01:54,850
were among the first killed.

31
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:58,936
Around a hundred mutineers
went on to the Tanglin camp,

32
00:01:59,016 --> 00:02:04,925
where 309 German internees were held,
and about ten guards died in the attack.

33
00:02:04,925 --> 00:02:10,200
Some 35 Germans escaped, though
most did not join the mutineers.

34
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:13,100
Civilians were among the dead.

35
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:15,928
Through the night, the mutineers besieged

36
00:02:16,008 --> 00:02:18,775
the bungalow of the commanding officer.

37
00:02:18,775 --> 00:02:22,300
Martial law was declared the next day.

38
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:25,958
The Straits Times reported
that the civilians killed

39
00:02:26,038 --> 00:02:30,917
by the mutineers were buried at
Bidadari Cemetery the next afternoon,

40
00:02:30,997 --> 00:02:33,800
with the Bishop of Singapore assisting.

41
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:38,100
The coffins were brought in
two motor lorries and a hearse.

42
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:40,820
The paper's list covered civilians only,

43
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:44,400
not those killed helping
to put down the outbreak.

44
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:47,901
Sources today put the
number of soldiers and

45
00:02:47,981 --> 00:02:52,375
civilians killed at somewhere
between about 36 and 44.

46
00:02:52,375 --> 00:02:55,250
Help came from outside the island.

47
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:58,024
On the 17th of February, ships arrived:

48
00:02:58,104 --> 00:03:01,977
the French cruiser Montcalm,
the Russian cruiser Orel,

49
00:03:02,057 --> 00:03:05,350
and the Japanese cruisers
Otowa and Tsushima.

50
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:10,260
Landing parties from the ships
went ashore, roughly 75 Japanese,

51
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:12,989
22 Russians and 190 French marines,

52
00:03:13,069 --> 00:03:18,681
joining the Singapore Volunteer Corps,
the Malay States Volunteer Rifles,

53
00:03:18,761 --> 00:03:22,425
police, and troops sent
by the Sultan of Johor.

54
00:03:22,425 --> 00:03:25,970
By the evening of the 17th of February,

55
00:03:26,050 --> 00:03:29,025
432 mutineers had been captured.

56
00:03:29,025 --> 00:03:31,707
The Shropshire Light Infantry arrived

57
00:03:31,788 --> 00:03:34,550
from Rangoon on the 20th of February.

58
00:03:34,550 --> 00:03:37,329
Some of the mutineers had fled into Johor,

59
00:03:37,409 --> 00:03:40,200
where a group was picked up the same day.

60
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:43,272
The fighting was largely
over within a week,

61
00:03:43,352 --> 00:03:47,650
though a last skirmish was
reported on the 25th of February.

62
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:51,200
More than 200 men were court-martialled.

63
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:56,024
Forty-seven were executed, about
64 were transported for life,

64
00:03:56,104 --> 00:04:01,325
and roughly 73 were imprisoned for
between seven and twenty years.

65
00:04:01,325 --> 00:04:05,615
The executions took place
outside Outram Road Prison,

66
00:04:05,695 --> 00:04:10,725
in public, although public
executions had ended in the 1890s.

67
00:04:10,725 --> 00:04:14,400
The Straits Times described
the largest batch.

68
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:18,570
On the 25th of March, the
sentences of 45 mutineers were

69
00:04:18,650 --> 00:04:22,744
read out in public outside
the prison walls, and the 22

70
00:04:22,824 --> 00:04:27,225
who had been condemned to death
were executed on the spot.

71
00:04:27,225 --> 00:04:30,292
The paper estimated a crowd of 15,000

72
00:04:30,372 --> 00:04:33,775
watching from the slopes of Sepoy Lines.

73
00:04:33,775 --> 00:04:38,672
The firing party was 110 men of
the Singapore Volunteer Corps,

74
00:04:38,752 --> 00:04:42,284
and the sentences were
translated into Malay,

75
00:04:42,364 --> 00:04:45,575
Chinese and Hindustani for the audience.

76
00:04:45,575 --> 00:04:48,800
Other accounts name a
different firing party,

77
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:52,625
and which men fired on
which day is still disputed.

78
00:04:52,625 --> 00:04:57,025
The full trial records were
sealed for about fifty years.

79
00:04:57,025 --> 00:05:00,864
The 5th Light Infantry left Singapore

80
00:05:00,944 --> 00:05:05,075
in July 1915 and was disbanded in 1922.

81
00:05:05,075 --> 00:05:09,253
Three years after the mutiny,
the colonial government created

82
00:05:09,333 --> 00:05:12,882
a Criminal Intelligence
Department, which became the

83
00:05:12,962 --> 00:05:17,150
Special Branch and, later, the
Internal Security Department.

84
00:05:17,150 --> 00:05:20,525
The mutiny left few marks on the city.

85
00:05:20,525 --> 00:05:24,632
There are memorial tablets to
the dead at the Victoria Memorial

86
00:05:24,712 --> 00:05:28,752
Hall and plaques at Saint Andrew's
Cathedral, and a few roads,

87
00:05:28,832 --> 00:05:33,550
such as Walton, Harper and Holt Roads,
are named for the people killed.

88
00:05:33,550 --> 00:05:38,850
I could find no memorial in Singapore
to the men who were executed.

89
00:05:38,850 --> 00:05:42,743
Historians still frame the
event differently, some as a

90
00:05:42,823 --> 00:05:47,005
colonial security failure,
others as part of a wider global

91
00:05:47,085 --> 00:05:51,275
war in which Indian soldiers
had their own reasons to act.

92
00:05:51,275 --> 00:05:54,531
Singapore treats racial
and religious tension

93
00:05:54,611 --> 00:05:57,725
as a matter for law as well as persuasion.

94
00:05:57,725 --> 00:06:02,452
Parliament repealed the Sedition
Act in 2021, but the Maintenance

95
00:06:02,532 --> 00:06:06,593
of Religious Harmony Act of
1990 lets the Minister issue

96
00:06:06,673 --> 00:06:11,548
restraining orders against religious
leaders, and a new Maintenance

97
00:06:11,628 --> 00:06:16,577
of Racial Harmony Act came into
force on the 15th of September 2026,

98
00:06:16,657 --> 00:06:20,650
replacing the race-related
offences in the Penal Code.

99
00:06:20,650 --> 00:06:25,850
Housing and electoral rules are
also designed to mix communities.

100
00:06:25,850 --> 00:06:30,503
The state usually explains this
approach by pointing to the

101
00:06:30,583 --> 00:06:35,878
racial riots of 1964 and 1969, and
supporters see it as a safeguard

102
00:06:35,958 --> 00:06:40,450
against a danger that has
already been shown to be real.

103
00:06:40,450 --> 00:06:44,424
Critics, including scholars
and United Nations experts,

104
00:06:44,504 --> 00:06:49,075
have questioned what such laws
mean for freedom of expression.

105
00:06:49,075 --> 00:06:54,550
I found no official speech
that cites 1915 in this debate.

106
00:06:54,550 --> 00:06:59,386
It is an earlier episode, though,
in which questions of religion,

107
00:06:59,466 --> 00:07:03,625
community and loyalty collided
with colonial authority.

108
00:07:03,625 --> 00:07:07,702
Why it matters today: The mutiny
is one of the few times the

109
00:07:07,782 --> 00:07:11,651
island's colonial order was
seriously shaken from inside,

110
00:07:11,731 --> 00:07:15,750
yet it is far less remembered
than the wars that followed.

111
00:07:15,750 --> 00:07:18,831
Its lessons are still
argued over, from how a

112
00:07:18,911 --> 00:07:22,063
government treats the
soldiers it relies on to

113
00:07:22,143 --> 00:07:25,375
how it balances order
against free expression.
